his one.\n```\n\nThat's fine.\n\nHmm, one more: test_06 also requires the Releases section of CONTRIBUTING to carry the Owner's cadence amendment verbatim: \"a release that changes no requirement does not count toward the monthly cadence\". At base, is it present? test_06 is green at base... Let me check the base clause: if uncut, it asserts the problems mention \"no entry for\" or \"does not say the release changed no\". Hmm, at base the newest changelog entry is 0.9.8, version 0.9.8, so `uncut` is True \u2192 only asserts some problem exists. Then it plants the amendment into a copy by replacing \"At most one release a month while the version starts with 0\" and asserts measure_cadence reports no \"amendment\" problem. That works regardless of whether the subject carries the amendment because the plant inserts it.\n\nBut once cut, `self.assertEqual(problems, [], \"\\n\".join(problems))` for measure_cadence(self.subject), which requires the amendment in the Releases section AND the changelog claim. So I MUST insert the amendment into CONTRIBUTING.md's Releases section!\n\nLet me re-read: CONTRIBUTING Releases section:\n\n```\n## Releases\n\nAt most one release a month while the version starts with 0, quarterly after 1.0. Versions follow\nSemantic Versioning 2.0.0; `SPEC.md` section 8 says what counts as a major change. Every release is a\ngit tag, and is cited by its commit.\n```\n\nNo amendment present at base. So I must add it. The amendment text: \"a release that changes no requirement does not count toward the monthly cadence\". It's matched with `carried_verbatim`, which tolerates wrapping. So I can add: \"A release that changes no requirement does not count toward the monthly cadence.\" \u2014 note case: `carried_verbatim` checks `needle in \" \".join(haystack.split())` \u2014 case-sensitive! The needle is lowercase \"a release that changes no requirement...\". If I capitalize \"A release...\", the substring won't match!\n\nHmm. Careful. `carried_verbatim(releases, CADENCE_AMENDMENT)` \u2192 `if needle in \" \".join(haystack.split())`. Case-sensitive. So I need the exact lowercase string somewhere. That means the sentence must start lowercase, or be embedded mid-sentence.\n\nSo write: \"At most one release a month while the version starts with 0, quarterly after 1.0: a release that changes no requirement does not count toward the monthly cadence.\"\n\nThat embeds the amendment lowercase mid-sentence. But careful with the second reading (`joined` with newlines replaced): the first check `needle in \" \".join(haystack.split())` normalizes all whitespace to single spaces, so wrapping is fine.\n\nBut wait \u2014 does inserting this break test_07's (d) arm? Sentence-level comparison. The base has the paragraph:\n\n\"At most one release a month while the version starts with 0, quarterly after 1.0. Versions follow Semantic Versioning 2.0.0; `SPEC.md` section 8 says what counts as a major change. Every release is a git tag, and is cited by its commit.\"\n\nsentences() flushes at blank lines and headings; so the Releases section body: lines joined \u2192 one unit starting \"At most one release a month...\". UNSTABLE_PREFIXES includes \"At most one release a month\". So in the (d) arm, for base units starting with that prefix, it checks `head = \" \".join(s.split()[:8])` = \"At most one release a month while the version starts\" and requires `head in _body_without_versions(n)` for some now unit. If I edit that sentence, the head must survive: \"At most one release a month while the version starts with 0, quarterly after 1.0: a release that ...\" \u2014 head is \"At most one release a month while the version starts\" (8 words: At(1) most(2) one(3) release(4) a(5) month(6) while(7) the(8)) \u2192 present. \u2713\n\nHmm wait: `\" \".join(s.split()[:8])` on the base sentence gives \"At most one release a month while the version starts\" \u2014 yes 8 tokens: At, most, one, release, a, month, while, the. And the new text contains that 